entries.text:
On top of the chain's limit, Kawase applies rules of its own:

- a per-payment cap;
- new recipients: for the first hours after you add someone, the allowance and automations stay off that address;
  your own payment still goes through, with a test amount suggested;
- allowlist only: the agent pays only people on your list;
- a temporary raise: more for a while, then back by itself.

Some requests are unusual enough to stop and show you why, even inside your limits: more than half your balance to
someone new, a trade whose price impact is over your rule, or leverage above 5×. The card shows a brake panel;
it's your call, and approving goes ahead. An automation that hits the brake waits in Needs you instead.

"Long Bitcoin 2x with 10 dollars", or the Long/Short screen. Choosing a side drafts a real plan at once ($10, 2×), so
the ticket opens with live numbers:

- your margin and the leveraged size, with presets and Max;
- a leverage ruler capped by the market, with a tick at every half step;
- the liquidation price and how far away it is, the account's health, the borrow rate and the network fee;
- the route, and a risk line.

You confirm by sliding (mint for a long, red for a short), then Face ID. The ticket follows the transaction from
checking to final. Any change to the plan resets the slide, so you never confirm numbers you didn't see.

- a daily budget and a per-call cap;
- ask me first above the cap: the purchase waits on a consent card in chat;
- the categories Kawase may buy from;
- blocked sellers: tap any purchase to block its seller.

Tightening applies at once; loosening asks for Face ID. A refusal names the rule that stopped it.

Home shows a few cards that deserve a look, computed from your real balances on the active network:

- idle USDC above what you spend, next to a vault that could pay it all back;
- one of your payment requests about to expire;
- a bridge that hasn't arrived 15 minutes after it should have;
- a big move in a token you hold (5% in a day, 0.5% for a currency stablecoin, at least $10 held);
- a margin position whose health is under 1.25 or within a 10% move of liquidation.

They're facts and options, never advice. Each card has one action. Dismiss hides a card for a week; don't show this
kind mutes it (and can be undone).

Your wallet has one address on both networks, and it takes any token on Arc. Add money gathers every way in:

- Buy with a card (Buy);
- From an exchange: the exchange's logo and its own steps (Coinbase through Base, then Bring to Arc);
- From another chain: USDC waiting at your address elsewhere, brought to Arc with no gas;
- Receive: your address and its QR code;
- Test USDC on testnet.

Money that arrives shows up in Activity and the inbox, can wake your automations, and offers
Save sender as a contact.

Tap any token, in Markets, on Home or in a chat card, to open its page:

- its mark, price and change, and a chart you can scrub with ranges from one hour to all time;
- what you hold of it;
- market cap, liquidity, volume, holders, fully diluted value and the contract (copy it in one tap);
- Safety: a verdict with its reasons, the checks from GoPlus (honeypot, buy and sell taxes, owner powers) and any
  look-alikes, linked;
- Buy · Sell, and a More sheet that lists every other action, each with the reason when it isn't available.

Home shows every token your address holds on Arc and on Ethereum, Base, Arbitrum, OP, Polygon and Avalanche, each with
its real mark and value. Kawase hides what would mislead you:

- an impostor (a stablecoin's or a well-known symbol at another address, or with other decimals) is hidden;
- a token with no price and no pool goes to Hidden;
- an unpriced token never counts toward your total.

Every buy passes your token rules first:

- impersonators and honeypots are blocked;
- an unverified token is limited to $50 a trade and $200 a day;
- price impact may be at most 5% for anything that isn't a stablecoin.

A blocked trade says which rule stopped it and what would change the answer.

Download Kawase for Mac (Apple silicon, about 2 MB), open the disk image
and drag Kawase to Applications.

Not yet notarized by Apple: the first time, right-click Kawase → Open. On macOS 15 and later, open it once, then go to
System Settings → Privacy & Security and choose Open Anyway. This is a preview build, not a signed release.

It is the live web app in a native window, so it is always the current release with nothing to update:

- the title bar takes Kawase's colours and follows your light or dark mode;
- copy, paste and the usual menus work; ⌘+ and ⌘− zoom;
- kawase:// links (the same as on the phone) open the matching page in the window, for example kawase://r/… opens
  a payment request;
- sign-in windows open inside the app, and any other site (an explorer, these docs) opens in your browser;
- the page gets no access to your computer beyond the window it runs in.

Voice inside the desktop app hasn't been checked yet; use the web app in a browser, or your phone, for voice.

Windows and Linux builds come from the same source (apps/desktop); they haven't been built yet.
